	abstract = {This study aims to evaluate the impact of digital technology adoption on efficiency, transparency and accountability in zakat management, with a focus on the younger generation and highly educated individuals. Using quantitative analysis of 400 respondents in Tangerang city, the findings show that digital zakat adoption is more dominant among the 18-34 age group and respondents with a bachelor’s degree or higher education. This group is more prepared to use digital platforms because of better access to technology and a greater understanding of its benefits. However, digital literacy gaps and limited access remain major challenges, particularly for older adults and low-income communities. The findings highlight that technology adoption, zakat ecosystem strengthening, and community involvement significantly influence operational efficiency and transparency in zakat management. This research contributes to the literature on zakat digitalization and offers strategic recommendations for zakat institutions to optimize technology adoption, emphasizing an inclusive approach that considers the demographic characteristics of users. Collaboration among zakat institutions, the government, and technology providers is essential to ensure successful technology integration and to meet the public’s demand for more transparent and efficient zakat services. © (c) 2026 The Authors. This work is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-NoDerivatives 4.0 International License.},
